    "romaji": "kaze wa mou naorimashita ka?",
    "ja": "風邪はもう治りましたか？",
    "english": "Have you got over your cold?"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "c-tsukare-ga-toreru",
   "romaji": "tsukare ga toreru",
   "polite": "tsukare ga toremasu",
   "english": "shake off tiredness, feel rested",
   "ja": "疲れが取れる",
   "kana": "つかれがとれる",
   "politeJa": "疲れが取れます",
   "noun": "tsukare",
   "particle": "ga",
   "verb": "toreru",
   "verbJa": "取れる",
   "verbSense": "come off, come out (a button, a stain); also can get (potential of toru)",
   "englishVerb": "recover",
   "trap": "Not tsukare ga naoru: tiredness isn't cured, it 'comes off' (toreru).",
   "partners": [
    "itami (pain)",
    "shimi (stain)",
    "botan (button)"
   ],
   "flags": [],
   "use": "Most often heard in the negative: nete mo tsukare ga torenai (I sleep and still feel tired). After a bath, a massage or a holiday it's tsukare toreta, and ga often drops.",
   "notes": "Tsukare o toru is doing something about it: onsen de tsukare o toru (soak it away at a hot spring). Tsukare ga nukenai (the tiredness won't leave) is also common.",
   "example": {
    "romaji": "nete mo, zenzen tsukare torenai.",
    "ja": "寝ても、全然疲れ取れない。",
    "english": "Even after sleeping, I can't shake the tiredness."
   },
